# Project constraints

Reuse what already exists. Keep every page very fast to load. Write code a person with less coding knowledge can open, understand, and change.

## Reuse components and styles

Before adding markup, a class, or a color, look in `src/components/` and `src/styles/`.

- Use an existing component (`Hero`, `ProjectCard`, `ProjectList`, `Prose`, `Seo`, `BaseLayout`). Add a prop when the same component needs a small variation.
- Colors, type, spacing, and motion come from `src/styles/tokens.css` (`--color-*`, `--type-*`, `--space-*`, `--dur-*`). Shared classes live in `src/styles/global.css` (`.site-shell`, `.project-card`, and the rest).
- Add a new component or class only when nothing current fits. Shared patterns go in `global.css`; classes used by one component go in that component’s `<style>` block.

```astro
<!-- Reuse the token and the shared class -->
<h2 class="project-card__title">{title}</h2>
```

```css
/* New color or space belongs in tokens.css, then get used by name */
color: var(--color-ink);
margin-top: var(--space-4);
```

## Very fast load

The page should arrive as plain HTML and one stylesheet.

- Stay with Astro components. Skip React, Vue, and Svelte, and skip `client:*`, unless a control cannot work without JavaScript.
- Keep styles in `global.css`, which already imports `tokens.css` and `reset.css`. Skip extra stylesheets, CSS-in-JS, icon fonts, and extra web fonts.
- Photos go through Astro `<Image>` with set widths. The first image on screen loads eagerly. Every later image is lazy.
- Skip animation libraries, analytics, and new packages when CSS or a few lines of HTML can do the job.

## Easy for a person to edit

- One file shows one thing on the page. Name the file after that thing: `ProjectCard.astro`.
- Write ordinary HTML and CSS class names in everyday words: `project-card__title`.
- Keep any logic in a few lines at the top of the file. Props have plain names (`title`, `summary`, `year`).
- Repeat a short block of HTML when it appears once or twice. Add a component when the same block is used in more than one place.
- When a trick needs a comment to explain it, replace the trick with the straightforward version.
